## THE FULL ENCLOSING FUNCTIONS AS THEY STAND NOW — every exit path and the common tail

```c
X(rldimi)
{
	uint32_t iw = ic->arg[0];
	int rs = (iw >> 21) & 31, ra = (iw >> 16) & 31;
	int sh = ((iw >> 11) & 31) | ((iw & 2) << 4);
	int mb = ((iw >> 6) & 31) | (iw & 0x20);
	int rc = ic->arg[0] & 1;
	int m, n = sh;
	uint64_t tmp, s = cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[rs];
	/*  TODO: Fix this, its performance is awful:  */
	while (n-- != 0) {
		int b = (s >> 63) & 1;
		s = (s << 1) | b;
	}
	m = mb; tmp = 0;
	do {
		tmp |= ((uint64_t)1 << (63-m));
		m = (m + 1) & 63;
	} while (m != ((64 - sh) & 63));
	cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra] &= ~tmp;
	cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra] |= (tmp & s);
	if (rc)
		update_cr0(cpu, cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra]);
}

X(rlwimi)
{
	uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(ic->arg[0]);
	MODE_uint_t ra = reg(ic->arg[1]);
	uint32_t iword = ic->arg[2];
	int sh = (iword >> 11) & 31;
	int mb = (iword >> 6) & 31;
	int me = (iword >> 1) & 31;
	int rc = iword & 1;

	tmp = sh ? ((tmp << sh) | (tmp >> (32-sh))) : tmp;

	/*  A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word.  */
	if (mb > me)
		ra = (ra & 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp << 32);

	for (;;) {
		uint64_t mask;
		mask = (uint64_t)1 << (31-mb);
		ra &= ~mask;
		ra |= (tmp & mask);
		if (mb == me)
			break;
		mb ++;
		if (mb == 32)
			mb = 0;
	}
	reg(ic->arg[1]) = ra;
	if (rc)
		update_cr0(cpu, ra);
}
```

## THE QUESTIONS — in the order I most want them answered

1. **`rldimi`: is `(64 - sh) & 63` the correct loop termination?** The architectural mask is
   `MASK(MB, 63-SH)`, so the last bit set should be `63-sh` and the loop should stop once `m` has
   advanced past it. **Verify against Book I's rldimi RTL**, and say what the pre-fix `63 - sh`
   computed once the `while (sh-- != 0)` loop had left `sh == -1`. Quote the RTL.
2. **`rldimi` boundary: what happens when `mb == ((64 - sh) & 63)`?** My reading is that the
   `do`/`while` sets bit `mb`, advances, and then runs all the way round to set **all 64 bits**,
   terminating when `m` returns — which is the correct full-wrap mask. **Confirm or refute by
   tracing it.** If it instead sets one bit and stops, that is a live defect in my fix.
3. **`rldimi`: `sh` is now read AFTER the rotate loop, where before the loop consumed it.** Confirm
   no other use of `sh` in this function depended on the old post-loop value, and that `n = sh`
   captures it before any modification.
4. *** `rlwimi` IN 32-BIT MODE — the question I am least sure of. *** `MODE_uint_t ra` is
   **32-bit** when this file is compiled for 32-bit PowerPC. So
   `ra = (ra & 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp << 32)` assigns a 64-bit expression to a 32-bit
   object and **the high half is discarded**. My belief is that this is correct-by-accident — a
   32-bit implementation has no high half to write — **but "correct by accident" is not a warrant.**
   Is it correct? Is it correct for the right reason? Would an explicit `#ifdef MODE64` or a comment
   be the honest form? **Check how this file handles the 32/64 split elsewhere and follow its
   idiom** rather than inventing one.
5. **`rlwimi`: is the ORDERING right?** The high-half write happens BEFORE the mask loop, and the
   loop then reads and writes `ra`'s low 32 bits only (`mask` is `1 << (31-mb)`, `mb` in `[0,31]`).
   **Confirm the loop cannot disturb the high half**, and that no path reaches `reg(ic->arg[1]) = ra`
   with the high half half-written.
6. **`rlwimi`: does `if (mb > me)` exactly characterise the wrapping case?** MB and ME here are the
   5-bit fields; the architectural mask is `MASK(MB+32, ME+32)`. **Is `mb > me` equivalent to
   `MB+32 > ME+32`, and are the `mb == me` and `mb == me+1` boundaries on the correct side?** Trace
   at least those two.
7. **`update_cr0(cpu, ra)` is now called with the possibly-high-half-written value.** Was it before?
   **Does the architecture want CR0 computed on the full 64-bit RA or the low word?** Cite Book I.
8. **WHAT IS THE SMALLEST EDIT THAT BREAKS EITHER FIX AND STILL PASSES `ppc_rldicl_probe.py`?**
   That probe is in your permitted set — read what it actually asserts. **Name the clause-(d) mutant
   for each fix** (guard kept, effect nulled) and say whether the probe would catch it. A row whose
   channel cannot vary under its named mutant is vacuous by construction.
9. **Do these two fixes belong in ONE commit or TWO?** They are different instructions and different
   defects in the same file. This project's scope rule joins two sites only if same device/semantic
   class, one fix design, same detector, no new oracle.

Answer in full. Do not write any file; return the whole answer and I will save it. **An honest "the
files do not settle this" is a real answer — and "your fix is wrong" is the most valuable one.**

